#### 8. Exception Handling in Tests
**Ref:** testsmells.org — *Exception Handling*
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` (lines 110–118)

```csharp
try
{
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

This is a capture-and-assert pattern (functional but clunky). MSTest provides `Assert.ThrowsException<T>` for this exact purpose.
**Risk:** If someone removes the `Assert.Fail`, the test silently passes when no exception is thrown.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order();

    var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
        () => processor.ProcessOrder(order));

    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

